  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations to Rhode Island totaled $615M in FY2026 to date. Obligations were down 32.1% year over year. Award activity produced 3,747 awards with an average award size of $164K. The Department of Defense dominated buying, obligating $503M, while the Department of Veterans Affairs was the next-largest buyer at $52M. Engineering Services was the top industry with $232M in obligations and Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing followed with $71.4M. RAYTHEON COMPANY led vendors with $80.5M, followed by SEACORP, LLC at $69.9M and RITE-SOLUTIONS, INC. at $55.7M. Historical context shows FY26 obligations at $615M compared with a high point of $1.2B in FY22 and FY24 and a FY25 total of $906M. Current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is ongoing, so the FY2026 to date figures reflect obligations recorded so far in the fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What explains the FY2026 to date decline in obligations?",
        "body": "Obligations were down 32.1% year over year, which accounts for the lower FY2026 to date total of $615M compared with recent years. The Department of Defense remains the primary buyer, obligating $503M, so shifts in DoD pacing can strongly affect statewide totals. Historical comparisons show FY25 at $906M and FY24 at $1.2B, indicating FY2026 activity is below recent fiscal-year totals. Current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is ongoing, so final year totals may differ."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies, vendors, and industries are concentrated in Rhode Island federal obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency with $503M in FY2026 to date, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs at $52M and the Department of Homeland Security at $16.8M. RAYTHEON COMPANY is the largest vendor with $80.5M, followed by SEACORP, LLC at $69.9M and RITE-SOLUTIONS, INC. at $55.7M. Engineering Services is the largest industry with $232M in obligations, and Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing is the next-largest industry with $71.4M. Top industry and vendor concentration shows a strong defense and engineering presence in the state's federal contracting."
      }
    ]
  },
